language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ Who killed Agatha: one of Agatha (person 1), the butler (person 2) and Charles (person 3) killed her; the killer hates the victim and is no richer than the victim.
$ killer is the 0-based index of the person who killed Agatha (0 Agatha, 1 the butler, 2 Charles); persons are numbered from 1 inside the model
find killer : int(0..2)
$ hates[x,y] is true when person x hates person y
find hates : matrix indexed by [int(1..3), int(1..3)] of bool
$ richer[x,y] is true when person x is richer than person y
find richer : matrix indexed by [int(1..3), int(1..3)] of bool
branching on [killer, hates, richer]
such that
$ a killer always hates his victim, Agatha, and is no richer than her
hates[killer+1, 1] /\ !richer[killer+1, 1],
$ no one is richer than himself
forAll x : int(1..3) . !richer[x, x],
$ if x is richer than y, then y is not richer than x
forAll x, y : int(1..3) . (richer[x, y] -> !richer[y, x]),
$ Agatha hates everybody except the butler (person 2)
forAll x : int(1..3) . (hates[1, x] <-> x != 2),
$ Charles hates no one that Agatha hates
forAll x : int(1..3) . (hates[1, x] -> !hates[3, x]),
$ the butler hates everyone who is not richer than Agatha
forAll x : int(1..3) . (!richer[x, 1] -> hates[2, x]),
$ the butler hates everyone whom Agatha hates
forAll x : int(1..3) . (hates[1, x] -> hates[2, x]),
$ no one hates everyone
forAll p : int(1..3) . exists x : int(1..3) . !hates[p, x]
